sec.p:
For the past several decades, DNA has proven to be an exceptional biomaterial, characterized by its biocompatible, biodegradable, and highly programmable properties.[
1
] These features enable the creation of nanostructures with user‐defined shapes and sizes, displaying fully addressable surfaces for precise site‐specific conjugation of guest molecules. DNA's versatile properties make it useful for various applications in biomedical and biotechnological fields.[
2 , 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 , 7
] One widely used technique for creating DNA nanostructures is DNA origami,[
8
] in which a long single‐stranded DNA scaffold is glued together by hundreds of short‐staple strands into a target structure through a thermal annealing process. In most traditional origami nanostructures, all scaffold and staple strands are arranged based on antiparallel crossovers (AX). Its counterpart, parallel crossovers (PX), are underdeveloped in DNA origami systems due to its topological complexity[
9 , 10 , 11
] and therefore more challenging to assemble. Only a few studies explored PX‐based origami, including those focusing on paranemic crossover‐based single‐strand routing[
12
] and unidirectional scaffold routing with partial parallel crossovers.[
13
]

In spite of limited studies of PX, PX‐based DNA nanostructures showed unique features and advantages. For example, previous studies reported the enhanced nuclease resistance of the PX‐based small DNA motifs due to its unique folding pathways,[
11 , 12 , 13 , 14
] providing a potential control of the structural integrity of small DNA nanostructures in biological environments. Another PX‐related feature is their unique topologies that allow single‐stranded routing of DNA.[
10 , 12 , 15 , 16
] These structures were demonstrated with high structural integrity due to their intramolecular folding and allowed the use of a low‐cost, high‐throughput biological technique[
12
] to produce the DNA.

These rankings can be primarily explained by two key factors: (1) for the same crossover type (PX or AX), increasing the number of the central staples and elongating the hybridization lengths of central staples significantly improves the relative formation yield of the origami design. This improvement is due to the increased local thermodynamic stability of the central staple domain, which, with higher melting temperatures, leads to the predominant formation of the designed origami at the early stage of thermal treatment from high to low temperature.[
17 , 18
] (2) for the designs with the same hybridization lengths, AX conformations are generally preferred over PX conformations due to the reduced topological challenges and lower entropic cost associated with folding. This preference likely arises because the AX conformations of both scaffold and staple crossovers more closely resemble the naturally adopted Holliday junction with a 60° angle, making it more difficult to transition to the PX conformation. Therefore, AX conformation is expected to be more favored as the staples bind more rapidly and at a shorter distance to the scaffold compared to the PX conformation. Interestingly, our results show that this barrier to adopting PX conformations can be overcome with appropriate adjustments in staple thermodynamics. This is evident in the ranking of P3 > A5,6,4, indicating that our PX origami designs are comparable to the AX designs and represent viable and competitive structures.

To gain deeper insights into this system and improve the interpretation of the origami rankings from the competitive folding experiments, we assessed the thermal stability of the two strongest (A3 and P3) and two weakest (A4 and P4) AX and PX origami designs individually (Figure S17, Supporting Information). Each design was first incubated at different temperatures for 3 h, ranging from 49 °C to 67 °C, and thermal degradation was analyzed using agarose gel electrophoresis (details in Section SI‐7, Supporting Information). The gel results revealed similar thermal transition profiles for all four designs, with band shifts observed between 58 °C and 61 °C (Figure S17a, Supporting Information) and further narrowed down to 59 °C to 60 °C (Figure S17c, Supporting Information). The AFM images (Figure S17b,d, Supporting Information) confirm these results, showing disassembled structures at 67 °C and intact structures at 59 °C for the selected P4 and A3 designs, respectively. These thermal stability results reflect the overall “melting” behavior of the origami structures. However, since our designs differ only in the central staples, these overall melting data cannot capture localized stability differences. The data also highlight that competitive study offers a useful approach to examining binding and assembly preferences in specific regions of origami.[

20

] Our comparative results not only differentiate staple thermodynamics based on hybridization lengths but also reveal configuration preferences when hybridization lengths are identical (e.g., AX4 vs PX4).

In addition, we tested the nuclease resistance of these four origami designs (A3, A4, P3, and P4) through DNase I digestion. Overall, the origami exhibited comparable nuclease resistance, lasting up to 45 min for P3 and A3 designs and up to 60 min for P4 and A4, as shown in Figure S18 (Supporting Information). To investigate the effect of increased PX crossovers on the origami nuclease resistance, we also successfully assembled a new PX design, P7, with 50% AX and 50% PX system, featuring 11 scaffold PX crossovers and 88 staple PX crossovers (Figure S18c, Supporting Information). The nuclease degradation characterized through agarose gel and the AFM images of 45 min of digestion indicated a similar nuclease resistance comparable with A4 and P4 designs. This result can be attributed to two factors. First, the P7 origami design contains only 50% PX crossovers. In previous studies on PX motifs,[
11 , 12 , 13 , 14
] these configurations relied entirely on PX crossovers, leading to inherent topological entanglement, which complicates PX‐based assembly. In contrast, the P7 design incorporates ≈50% AX crossovers to accommodate effective scaffold routing, alternating AX and PX crossovers in different helical rows. Unlike motifs, which do not involve scaffold strands and thus avoid routing challenges, the P7 origami design reduces topological entanglement, making it easier to assemble while demonstrating nuclease resistance comparable to AX‐based designs. Second, the shape dependency of nuclease resistance plays a role. Previous studies have demonstrated that compact 3D configurations,[
11
] such as tubular structures, offer superior nuclease resistance, while the 2D topology of the current P7 origami design is more vulnerable to DNase I digestion due to its higher exposed surface area. Unlike earlier studies that primarily investigated the nuclease resistance of PX motifs, our findings reveal the complexity of this mechanism in full origami structures. These results suggest that a fully PX‐based origami design, featuring enhanced topological entanglement in a compact 3D configuration, could potentially achieve greater nuclease resistance, inspiring the development of such advanced systems.

Similarly, we conducted the competitive assembly tests between 8 AX‐PX pairs (Figure 3b,c). After annealing and characterization, we counted the formation yields of each AX and PX design in the competitive test. Based on the AFM images (Figure 3b,c; Figures S23 and S24, Supporting Information), we observed several interesting results. First, in the competition between long and short staples, designs incorporating long staples consistently outperformed those with short staples. Almost all origami formations with long staples, regardless of their specific arrangement (P3 in > A4 out, P3 out > A4 in, A3 in > P4 out, A3 out > P4 in). This may be due to the thermodynamic preference from the elongated staples during annealing. Second, for the competing structures with both long staples (A3 out vs P3 in, A3 in vs P3 out), AFM showed mostly misformed structures. This indicates a mixture of staples for both AX and PX configurations bind to the origami scaffold at a high temperature during annealing. Finally, results of the last two combinations of all short staples (P4 in vs A4 out, P4 out vs A4 in) demonstrated the importance of the locations of staples: the final structural configuration (AX or PX) relies on the staples near the central scaffold crossover – the short AX staples near center led to the dominant AX origami formation, and the short PX staples led to dominant PX origami formation. Figure 3d,e shows the AFM images, one of which is labeled for origami counting (PX = 468, AX = 188, misformed = 24) in the “P4 in versus A4 out” example. The impact of central staples in our study aligns with the previous simulation work[

21

] where these staples quickly decide the configuration through the zipping mechanism. In addition, comparing these two combinations (P4 in vs A4 out, P4 out vs A4 in), the higher percentage (87.8%) of AX origami formation than the PX origami formation (68.8%) can be attributed to the ease of AX formation.[

9
, 
20

] It is important to note that our designs follow the seam scaffold routing, allowing a similar zipping mechanism to the previous simulation study.[

21

] Overall, this folding pathway study highlights the important role of middle staples in determining the final origami structure configuration, bringing insights into guided origami formation mechanisms.

In this study, we explored a new experiment involving isothermal annealing of A3, A4, P3, and P4 with TANa buffer, containing Na+ instead of Mg2+ from our standard protocol. A previous study by Rossi‐Gendron et al[

22

] demonstrated an example of isothermally annealed origami nanostructures using TANa buffer. Inspired by this study, we tested to find out if our origami design can form isothermally with TANa buffer. The motivations for this isothermal study were from the potential advantages of using isothermal annealing, instead of applying the standard thermal treatment, that is: 1) enabling the study of the spontaneous, evolutive process of nanostructure formation and dynamic transformation to other structures at constant environmental conditions 2) opening up opportunities for potential applications in ambient or living systems 3) allowing the conjugation of nanostructures with temperature‐sensitive structures like proteins, leading to various hybrid nanostructures for biological applications. In the isothermal annealing procedure, the use of TANa buffer is critical since the TANa buffer prevents forming kinetically trapped structures of the origami, which could be enhanced in the use of buffer containing Mg2+.[

21

] In Figure

4b, the agarose gel result showed compact bands indicating possible formation of P3, A3, P4, and A4 origami annealed at 35 °C for four days in TANa 100 mm [NaCl] at 2.5 nm origami scaffold with 25 nm staples (10x the origami scaffold). As shown in the AFM images of Figure 4c, all four origami at 35 °C were able to form the target‐designed structures. Overall, these results demonstrated that these four AX and PX origami designs can be isothermally annealed at 35 °C, which is below their melting temperatures, with TANa buffer.

In summary, we synthesized 12 different AX and PX origami nanostructures by varying the central staple thermodynamics, systematically studied, and ranked them according to their relative yields in the 36 competitive annealing, leading to the four of the strongest and weakest origami (P3,4 and A3,4). These competitive test results demonstrated that through increasing the thermodynamic stability of the origami design via introducing more central staples and elongating the hybridization lengths of central staples, the strongest ranked PX origami (P3) can outcompete A5, 6, and 4. Furthermore, using the strongest and weakest origami, we investigated their folding pathway via middle staple arrangement and demonstrated its impact on the final origami configuration. Finally, we explored the isothermal assembly of P3, A3, P4, and A4 origami below their melting temperatures and their dynamic transformation between AX and PX conformations in two‐way directions (A4 to P3 and P4 to A3). These results promise our PX design's use in future studies that require isothermal or temperature‐sensitive environments in biomedical and bionanotechnological applications and bring about opportunities to explore the evolutive, dynamic changes between AX and PX conformations under the relatively stable temperature environment like in living organisms, which could further branch out to investigate AX and PX related biological events.[
19 , 24 , 25
] Overall, our study results demonstrated that our PX origami designs are reasonably stable as AX origami designs and serve as a foundation on which new variations of PX origami can easily be created using the traditional AX origami platform to exploit the benefits of using PX origami systems and to make novel PX/AX dynamic nanodevices.
